Some mutual fund share classes charge sales loads and 12b-1 fees that ETFs do not, though no-load index funds skip the sales load. A front-end load is paid when shares are bought and a back-end load when they are sold, while [12b-1 fees](https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/glossary/12b-1-fees) come out of fund assets and are capped by law at 1% of average net assets per year. ETFs carry no sales load but do carry trading costs, and a fund's loads and 12b-1 fees are listed in its [prospectus fee table](https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/general-resources/news-alerts/alerts-bulletins/investor-bulletins/mutual-fund-and-etf-fees-and-expenses-investor-bulletin), which does not show brokerage commissions.